<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Kobe</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Kobe is a port city in western <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/japan">Japan</a> on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/osaka-bay">Osaka Bay</a>, capital of Hyogo Prefecture. Its old harbour of Hyogo, known in the 12th century as Owada no Tomari, was developed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/taira-no-kiyomori">Taira no Kiyomori</a> and was a chief port for trade with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/china">China</a> until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/onin-war">Onin War</a>. Opened to foreign trade in 1868, Kobe became one of Japan's largest ports and was devastated by the Great Hanshin earthquake of 1995.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/kobe.jpg" alt="Kobe" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Balon Greyjoy · CC0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:20190901_Kobe_harbor-1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Sakamoto Ryōma first went to Katsu Kaishū intending to assassinate him for his pro-foreign views; instead Katsu won him over to his plan for rearmament, and Sakamoto followed him to the <b>Kobe</b> Naval Training Centre.</blockquote><a href="/movement/meiji-restoration">Meiji Restoration · At a glance</a></li><li><blockquote>A magnitude 6.9 earthquake struck the <b>Kobe</b> region before dawn, killing 6,434 people and destroying over 100,000 buildings.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/january-17">17 January in history</a></li><li><blockquote>Sakai grew as a port on Osaka Bay and became a terminus of the official trade with Ming China after the Onin War made the routes through <b>Hyogo</b> unsafe from about 1469.</blockquote><a href="/state/sakai">Sakai</a></li><li><blockquote>By the 17th century several Zaghawa chieftaincies had formed sultanates, among them <b>Kobe</b>, Dar Tuar and Dar Gala, in the semi-desert north of Darfur and Wadai.</blockquote><a href="/state/zaghawa-sultanates">Zaghawa sultanates</a></li></ul></section>
